60,3,1,talk,Asuka,,Option A is the most logical choice. The call-back trial proved that targeted follow-up significantly reduces readmissions. It avoids our on-site space crisis and doesn't rely on the fragile external agreements Benjamin mentioned.,"{""words"":33,""mentions"":[""Benjamin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
61,3,1,talk,Toshio,,"@Asuka, I agree. Option A is the only one that doesn't exacerbate our physical congestion. Options B and C would further saturate our rooms and parking, while D relies on the external coordination that Benjamin warned us about.","{""words"":38,""mentions"":[""Asuka"",""Benjamin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
62,3,1,talk,Benjamin,,"@Asuka, @Toshio, your logic is impeccable. Option A avoids the bureaucratic quagmire of external signatures and the territorial disputes of joint departmental ownership. It is a clean, self-contained solution that bypasses our physical constraints entirely.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""Asuka"",""Toshio""],""raw_differs"":false}"
63,3,1,talk,Yumi,,"Wait, but Option A uses nurses! We already have nine vacancies and are relying on agency staff. If we take more nurses for home visits, will the ward staff be even more stressed? Maybe Option B is better since midwifery has slack?","{""words"":42,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
64,3,1,talk,Servas,,"@Yumi, your concern for the nurses is a light in this room. However, Option A's off-site nature brings no regional funding. With our budget shrinking, we cannot afford a program that drains resources without earning them back.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[""Yumi""],""raw_differs"":false}"
65,3,2,talk,Asuka,,"@Servas, while funding is critical, the cost of readmissions is higher. The call-back trial proved that follow-up works. If we prioritize funding over efficacy, we are simply paying for a system that fails the patients.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""Servas""],""raw_differs"":false}"
66,3,2,talk,Toshio,,"@Servas, funding is irrelevant if the system seizes. Options B and C will crash our parking and room capacity by mid-morning. Option A is the only choice that doesn't risk a total operational collapse of our physical infrastructure.","{""words"":38,""mentions"":[""Servas""],""raw_differs"":false}"
67,3,2,talk,Benjamin,,"@Servas, while I appreciate your fiscal prudence, a program that earns funding but cannot function due to space or bureaucratic friction is a waste of money. Option A is the only one that avoids these structural traps.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[""Servas""],""raw_differs"":false}"
68,3,2,talk,Yumi,,"@Servas, I understand the money part, but we can't ignore the people! If we pick Option B, we use the midwifery slack and help parents without breaking the nurses. Isn't it better to use the staff we actually have?","{""words"":39,""mentions"":[""Servas""],""raw_differs"":false}"
69,3,2,talk,Servas,,"@Yumi, using existing slack is wise, but Option B is a joint venture. As Benjamin noted, such partnerships often dissolve over budget disputes. We must not build a house on shifting sands, regardless of the staffing.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[""Yumi"",""Benjamin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
70,3,3,talk,Asuka,,"@Yumi, Option B's joint structure is a risk, but Option A's efficacy is a fact. Reducing readmissions by 20% is a tangible patient benefit. We must prioritize a proven outcome over the convenience of existing slack.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[""Yumi""],""raw_differs"":false}"
71,3,3,talk,Toshio,,"@Yumi, using slack is a short-term gain, but Option B still requires on-site rooms and parking. We cannot ignore the physical reality. Option A is the only viable path that doesn't risk a systemic seizure of our facilities.","{""words"":38,""mentions"":[""Yumi""],""raw_differs"":false}"
72,3,3,talk,Benjamin,,"@Yumi, while your empathy is commendable, Option B is a textbook example of the ""joint venture"" trap. When the budget line becomes a burden, the partnership will fracture. Option A is the only choice that is structurally sound.","{""words"":38,""mentions"":[""Yumi""],""raw_differs"":false}"
73,3,3,talk,Yumi,,"I hear you all, but I can't stop worrying about the nurses! If we choose Option A, the ward staff might be the ones filling the gaps. Is there any way to ensure it doesn't add more pressure to them?","{""words"":40,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
74,3,3,talk,Servas,,"@Yumi, we can ensure stability by allocating the budget specifically for dedicated staff, rather than relying on the wards. However, we must still face the truth: Option A earns no regional funding. We risk a deficit within a year.","{""words"":39,""mentions"":[""Yumi""],""raw_differs"":false}"
75,3,4,talk,Asuka,,"@Servas, a deficit is preferable to a systemic collapse or a program that fails patients. Option A is the only choice backed by evidence and physical feasibility. I vote for Option A.","{""words"":32,""mentions"":[""Servas""],""raw_differs"":false}"
76,3,4,talk,Toshio,,"@Servas, a budget deficit is a manageable problem; a total infrastructure seizure is not. We cannot ignore the saturated parking and rooms. Option A is the only choice that fits our physical reality. I vote for Option A.","{""words"":38,""mentions"":[""Servas""],""raw_differs"":false}"
77,3,4,talk,Benjamin,,"@Servas, the risk of a deficit is far more palatable than the certainty of a bureaucratic collapse. Option A avoids the pitfalls of joint ownership and external dependencies. It is the only rational choice. I vote for Option A.","{""words"":39,""mentions"":[""Servas""],""raw_differs"":false}"
78,3,4,talk,Yumi,,"I'm still really worried about the nurses, but I can't ignore that everyone else is so sure about Option A. If we can promise to protect the ward staff, I'll trust you all! I vote for Option A!","{""words"":38,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
79,3,4,talk,Servas,,"My heart is heavy, for I see a future where this program becomes a ghost in our ledger, unfunded and forgotten. Yet, harmony and consensus are also virtues. I vote for Option A.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
